1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://www.ra.ee/dgs/_purl.php?shc=EAA.1216.1.285:79?480,888,97,32,0 Kuusalu kogudus. Personaalraamat VIII (Loo, Kõnnu, Kolga, Kiiu, ...; EAA.1216.1.285; 1900-1930]
2 CONT * Sünd: [http://www.ra.ee/dgs/_purl.php?shc=EAA.1216.1.31:125?1376,360,162,62,0 Loksa kabelikoguduses sündinute ja surnute ...; EAA.1216.1.31; 10.1906-05.1929] - vkj 03. detsember 1912
2 CONT
2 CONT * Vancouveri Eesti Selts; Läänekaare Postipoiss; Nr. 210 sügis 2007: https://www.yumpu.com/xx/document/read/6383521/siin-this-is-the-ves
2 CONT IN MEMORIAM - '''Leida Varma, December 16, 1912 - July 15, 2007'''
2 CONT Leida Varma was born December 16, 1912 at Kuusela [sic] in the village of Suurmaa [sic] which is in Loksa parish. Leida died at the age of 94 years on July 15, 2007 in Vancouver, Canada.
2 CONT Leida was the third of five children of father Jüri Varma and mother Madleena. She had two older sisters and one younger sister and brother. Soon after she finished Elementary School her mother died and Leida became the caretaker 
2 CONC of her younger siblings for quite a while. She had no opportunity to continue her formal schooling but when one of the older sisters returned home, she did get to Tallinn to take a hairdressing course.
2 CONT In the war years when Leida was a young woman, she experienced deep tragedy in her close-knit family. One of her older sisters with husband, two small sons and the younger sister were deported to Siberia in 1941. The rest of the f
2 CONC amily lived in agony; not knowing their fate, and all the while they too had to fear arrest. The one encouraging development for Leida in those years was her friendship with Linda and Veeliks Vaide, a friendship which continued in
2 CONC  Vancouver and lasted until their death.
2 CONT In 1944, when escape from the occupying communist forces was the only way to save one's life, Leida, and Linda joined the fleeing masses. Together they rode their bicycles to the coast where they managed to get onboard a ship for 
2 CONC Germany. In Germany they lived in DP camps the first couple of years after the war, then Leida was sent to England to work in a hospital. She entered Canada as an immigrant on August 16, 1951, landing in Halifax on T.S.S. "Columbi
2 CONC a", but continued straight to Vancouver to join her friends Linda and Veeliks who were here already.
2 CONT In Vancouver Leida worked at UBC Food Services in Fort Camp for about 26 years. She enjoyed the student atmosphere and made many friendships among the many, many students that passed through in those years. She could tell by looki
2 CONC ng at them how hungry they were and judge how big a serving they needed or how deep to dip the ladle to serve extra thick soup. For those who at at Fort Camp, and perhaps later elsewhere too, she was a friend and someone who cared
2 CONC , who showed interest, who added a bit of humour. It is good to listen to the stories by students who knew her.
2 CONT Once established in Vancouver, the highlights in Leida's life were visits to her family. One older sister had managed to get to Sweden, and in 1962 Leida first visited her. After that she went back many times to a kind of family r
2 CONC eunion in Sweden with participants even from Estonia. In 1992, when Estonia was free again, Leida returned to see the family and the places of her youth.
2 CONT The Estonian community in Vancouver knows Leida Varma well and will miss her; she was an active member and contributed to social functions, ~ she was loyal, helpful and graceful and everyone's friend; she was a generous giver and 
2 CONC a grateful person.
2 CONT Leida's closest friends remember that she loved flowers and animals, and that she was especially fascinated by the magic of the white nights of the Estonian summers.
2 CONT An image of such beauty is expressed by Marie Under in a poem about a summer's night in the countryside:
2 CONT ''Imagine a golden sky sprinkling yellow nuggets onto the treetops,''
2 CONT ''Imagine down below a silvery mist moistening the hayfield,''
2 CONT ''breathe in the fragrance of the reddish clover''
2 CONT ''and walk along lightly, step very softly.''
2 CONT ''Pause at the wild rose and admire its pink petals,''
2 CONT ''which slowly form a snow-like cover on the ground.''
2 CONT ''Observe the flowers, who like tired children, long to sleep.''
2 CONT ''Hear the bells of hers on homeward trails''
2 CONT ''and note the stillness, the evergrowing stillness of the world.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the poem in Estonian:
2 CONT ''Pilvist kulda puudele piisutab,''
2 CONT ''hõbeudu heinamaad niisutab.''
2 CONT ''Rammus punapäine ristikhein''
2 CONT ''lõhnab raskelt siin, kus ma vaikselt käin.''
2 CONT ''Kibuvitsalt on varisend roosat lund.''
2 CONT ''Lilled - kui väsind lapsed - ihkavad und.''
2 CONT ''Til-tal, til-tal, kari koju lääb,''
2 CONT ''siis vaikseks kõik, ikka vaiksemaks jääb.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juta Kitching
